2 CONT Guglielmo Aldobandesco
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1216 the vast county was divided into four parts: Ildebrandino Maggiore , Ildebrandino Minore , Bonifazio and Guglielmo , a prominent figure, so much so that Dante will remember him in the Divine Comedy , in canto XI of Purgato
2 CONC ry through the words of Oberto, son of Guglielmo, as the " great tosco ":
2 CONT
2 CONT "I was Latin, and born of a great deal of sheep: Guglielmo Aldobrandesco was my father; I don't know if his name was ever known. " 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------
2 CONT
2 CONT https://translate.google.com/translate?hl=en&sl=it&u=http://www.wandruszka-genealogie.eu/Antonio/Antonio_Upload/Aldobrandeschi.pdf&prev=search
2 CONT
2 CONT XXIII.8047372 Aldobrandeschi Guglielmo called "the Great Tosco" by Dante Alighieri, + autumn 1254. Extensive biography of Luciana MARCHETTI in the Biographical Dictionary of Italians 2 (1960): "Son of Ildebrandino (VIII), count of
2 CONC  Sovana and Pitigliano, on 22 October 1208 was left heir from his father, together with the brothers Ildebrandino maggiore, Bonifazio, Tommaso, Gemma, Margherita and Ildebrandino minore: which was the cause of disputes that ended 
2 CONC in October 1216, when the A. divided the county together with Ildebrandino maggiore, Ildebrandino minore and Bonifazio, also due to the pressure exerted by Orvieto. The 24th ag. 1224, the Author, also in the name of the brothers B
2 CONC onifazio and Ildebrandino minore (not of the major more mention was made between 1223 and 1224), concluded a treaty with the Sienese, to avoid the destruction, as well as the walls, charcoal pits and ditches, even the houses of Gr
2 CONC osseto, rebelled against Siena. It seems, however, that the Sienese in early September they also sacked the city, as shown by the Sienese chronicle of Paul of Tommaso Montauri (p. 189), who claims to have fallen Grosseto in the ha
2 CONC nds of the Sienese "a dì 8 of September, the day of Santa Maria ". From this moment the A. assumed an attitude hostile towards Siena, and in 1227, personally committed to the city, it is not known whether a train of Grosseto event
2 CONC s or an Aldobrandesque approach to Florence, he was taken and imprisoned. Released by intervention of Gregory IX, with whom Aldobrandeschi had pleaded the cause of Grosseto, the A., together with his brother Bonifazio, in 1228 too
2 CONC k part in the crusade of Frederick II, not giving up, however, from the continuous guerrilla warfare with the Sienese, between alternate events and continuous interventions by the Pope, who he wanted to pacify the Maremma territor
2 CONC y, as a land of the Church. Despite the looming of a pacification between Siena and Florence and the proclamation of the "great peace" of the 1235, with which the cardinal lacopo "de Pecoraria", bishop prenestino, promised himself
2 CONC  to quet the agitated Tuscan waters, in 1236 the hostilities between Siena and the A. they resumed their sign that the pope had to intervene to enforce the truce stipulated the year before. reason of the new contrast was given by 
2 CONC the non-payment of the fee to which the A. he was obliged, sin from 1221, as a sign of larvated vassalage towards Siena, to the Sienese Biccherna: la suspension of payment was due to the intention of the A. to claim damages during
2 CONC  the sack of Grosseto. And always from Siena, in that period, difficulties arose to the A., who had assumed the protection of his nephew Ildebrandino, son of Bonifazio: the widow of the latter, in fact, with Ugolino di Bertoldo di
2 CONC  Montorgiali and Ildebrandino di Guido Cacciaconti descended in arms against the. in April 1236. Following these actions by disturbance and for work - above all - of Buonatacca of lacquer Sansedoni, the A. lost some castles and se
2 CONC veral prisoners were captured. And the peace, stipulated in Siena on June 17th 1237, in the church of S. Cristoforo was a new confirmation of the treaty of 1221. In 1238 there. he came back to the lines, probably becoming a citize
2 CONC n, determining a new one invasion of lands and castles of the Aldobrandeschi area by the Sienese. The difficulties of the A., however, they increased in 1240 when, being governor and captain general for Tuscany Pandolfo di Fasanel
2 CONC la, its lands, as belonging to one of the most tenacious supporters of Tuscan Guelphism were invaded and almost completely occupied. THERE. he found himself having to face Frederick II's forces alone, which between 1241 and 1243 t
2 CONC hey succeeded in driving it away from much of the Albobrandesca and from Sovana itself. In 1243, however, the A., perhaps at the head of papal forces, reconquered Viterbo from the Church, resulting in one of the architects of the 
2 CONC victory smiled on that occasion by the Guelph forces. For these actions, the A. he obtained the reconfirmation of Montalto and facilities for the payment of some emphyteusis. 30 April 1251 - having not yet returned to the possessi
2 CONC on of the county, governed by the imperial vicars succeeded Pandolfo di Fasanella - made pacts with Florence by engaging among other things in hand over the ports of Talamone and Ercole with all the equipment. In May next, when Il
2 CONC debrandino of Santafiora went to Siena to agree with the republic, which had occupied Castiglion d'Orcia and Selvena, and-stayed again only against the powerful adversary, having the nephew with him a pact that bound to be an enem
2 CONC y of the enemies of Siena. In 1251, the war broke out between Florence, Lucca, Orvieto, on the one hand, and Siena, Pistoia and Pisa on the other, the A, which in 1253 was captain of the people in Orvieto - allied with the first a
2 CONC nd the peace of Stomennano near Monteriggioni the 11th of June. 1254 had returned from Siena the lands that had been taken away from it precedence both by the Sienese and by the imperial forces. Probably in the same year 1254 ther
2 CONC e. it went out, no longer showing its name in any document after that date. Dante ( Purg ., XI, vv. 58-6o) remembers him with words of esteem, even if pronounced by his son Umberto "
