2 CONT XXV.222944 Aldobrandeschi Ildebrandino (VII) Novello, living 1152/1179, + ante 5.1195, oo Maria Comitissa , daughter of Berardo (or Tancredi) of the Alberti Count of Prato and Orrabile (living 1164/1178). Extensive biography of Lu
2 CONC ciana MARCHETTI in the Biographical Dictionary of Italians 2 (1960): „Son of Uguccione; it seems that in 1155 he gave shelter in his lands to Arnaldo da Brescia, which was welcomed in the castle of one of its feudatories, the visc
2 CONC ount of Campagnatico, but which, following the armed intervention of Frederick I, had to be returned. Passed to the part of the emperor, on 20 March 1160 in the diet of St. Genesio swore allegiance to the duke Guelph, Marquis of T
2 CONC uscany, perhaps then receiving the title of Count Palatine and the confirmation of family possessions; according to Davidsohn, however, the title was granted to him in the spring 1163 (p. 745). In the same year, threatening Pisa t
2 CONC he war ad Ildebrandino for acts of piracy carried out along the Aldobrandesca coast against some of his ships, the count, even behind the advice of his mother, countess Gemma, rushed into the city a exculpate himself. A pact of al
2 CONC liance followed, for which Pisa had full lordship over the Tyrrhenian and the Aldobrandeschi the safety of their own coasts. THERE. he was awarded the title of standard-bearer. In May 1162 this pact was reconfirmed, while the foll
2 CONC owing July 9th it seems that the count participated in the diet again summoned to S. Genesio to do peace between Genoa and Pisa. In the spring of 1163 A. accompanied Rainald of Dassel, archbishop of Cologne and chancellor of the e
2 CONC mpire, in a tour through Tuscany in order to collect taxes and strengthen the authority of the emperor. 10 ag. 1164 Frederick I released him very large privileges, with which the A. he received imperial protection and the power to
2 CONC  coin coins; the Davidsohn maintains, moreover, that the ample privileges to the noble families of the Tuscany was released on September 28 and that the A. he was a little less favored for consideration to the Pisans, of whom Fede
2 CONC rico needed (p. 757). In 1169 he fought alongside Pisa against Lucca, at the siege of Agnano. In 1170, perhaps mayor and captain of Viterbo, it was at the head of a part of the Pisan army which, at war with Genoa and Lucca, besieg
2 CONC ed the castle of Motrone, which then fell. On 4 July 1171, on the occasion of the close alliance between Florence, Pisa, the bishop of Volterra and the count Alberto da Prato against Genoa, Lucca, Pistoia, Siena and Count Guido Gu
2 CONC erra, the A. he saw Pisa in his favor, yes reserved not to fight for Florence against him and against the bishop of Volterra, also pledging to favor the alliance between A. and Florence itself. March 28, 1172 was present in Siena 
