2 CONT Raymond V, Count of Toulouse
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond V (1134–1194) was count of Toulouse from 1148 until his death in 1194.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Alphonse-Jordan. When Alphonse died in the Holy Land in 1148, the county of Toulouse passed to his son Raymond, at the time 14 years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond was implicated in the Cathar crisis. In 1176, Guilhem, the Bishop of Albi organized the council of Lombers, attended by both clerics and princes, including Raymond of Trencavel, Raymond V, and his wife Constance, and the r
2 CONC epresentatives of the Cathars. The crisis later led to the Albigensian Crusade.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Family
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1154, Raymond married Constance of France, daughter of king Louis VI of France and his second wife Adélaide de Maurienne. Constance was the widow of Eustace IV of Boulogne. Because Raymond was related to her within prohibited d
2 CONC egrees, they were separated by ecclesiastical authority in 1165. They had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Raymond VI, who succeeded his father
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. Aubri, died 1180
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. Adelaide or Azalais of Toulouse, who married Roger II Trencavel in 1171 and died in 1199
2 CONT
2 CONT    4. Baldwin, born 1165, executed on the orders of Raymond VI in 1214
2 CONT
2 CONT    5. Laura died 1230 married Odo of Comminger d.1215
2 CONT
2 CONT His second wife was Richeza of Poland, widow of Ramon Berenguer II, Count of Provence.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT This page was last modified on 3 January 2010 at 08:49.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ramon Berenguer II, Count of Provence
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Ramon Berenguer II (Raymond Berengar) (c.1135 – 1166) was the count of Provence from 1144 to his death. His uncle, Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Barcelona, was the regent until 1157.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1144, Ramon's father, Count Berenguer Ramon, died in an offensive against Genoa and he inherited the county. He was immediately opposed by the family of Baux and it took the military action of his uncle, the count of Barcelona,
2 CONC  in 1147 to secure his throne. The war with the Baux continued until the count of Barcelona's death in 1162.
2 CONT
2 CONT In August of 1161, he had travelled to Turin with his uncle obtain the confirmation of his countship in Provence from the Emperor Frederick I, for Provence was legally a fief of the Holy Roman Empire. There he met Richeza of Polan
2 CONC d, the daughter of the exiled Polish high duke, Ladislaus II. He married her on 17 November and on the return journey, his uncle died.
2 CONT
2 CONT He resumed the war with Genoa, but died trying to take Nice in the spring of 1166. His daughter, Douce, succeeded him, while widow Richeza married Raymond V of Toulouse.
2 CONT
