1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tristan_de_Clermont-Lodève
2 CONT
2 CONT Barthélemy Guilhem, known as Tristan de Clermont-Lodève, known in Italy as "Tristano di Chiaromonte", born around 1380, in Languedoc, and died 3 January 1441[1],[2], was a Count of Copertino and a Lord of Clermont-Lodève, and the 
2 CONC father of the Queen Consort of Naples, Isabella of Taranto.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT The Guilhem castle in Clermont-l'Hérault (formerly Clermont-Lodève)
2 CONT
2 CONT The Royal and Pontifical Basilica of Santa Maria ad Nives in Copertino, which houses the mausoleum of Tristan of Clermont
2 CONT Barthélemy Guilhem was born around 1380 in Languedoc. He was the second son of Déodat II de Clermont-Lodève and his wife Isabelle de Roquefeuil. At a very young age he belonged to the entourage of Jacques II de Bourbon, Count of L
2 CONC a Marche and Castres, with whom he fought at the Battle of Nicopolis in 1396. He probably accompanied him in his other ventures and was one of the French lords who accompanied him when he married, in 1415, Queen Joan II of Naples[
2 CONC 3].
2 CONT
2 CONT Tristan de Clermont was one of the French lords on whom Jacques de Bourbon chose to rely to consolidate his power, which his wife challenged him in the Kingdom of Naples. On 1 November 1415, he married Catherine Orsini del Balzo, 
2 CONC daughter of Raymond des Ursins des Baux (Italian: Raimondo Orsini del Balzo) and Marie d'Enghien. Tristan gets off[4] of the lords of Clermont-Lodève who had contracted marriages with members of the House of Baux of the branch of 
2 CONC the Princes of Orange, his wife cannot be suspected of contracting a mésalliance.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie d'Enghien put in the couple's wedding basket the county of Cupertino, as her daughter's dowry, which included part of the lands and rights over Ceglie Messapica, Copertino, Galatone, Ginosa, Laterza, Leverano, and Matera[3],
2 CONC [5],[6].
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1419, Tristan de Clermont received Veglie as a fief, which he undertook to fortify[7].
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1423, he succeeded his brother Arnaud de Clermont at the head of the family lordship. This included, in addition to Clermont-Lodève, the viscounty of Nébouzon, the barony of Brusque, the lordship of Saint-Gervais and shares
2 CONC  in various co-lordships such as that of Boussagues[8].
2 CONT
2 CONT In particular, he made an agreement on 6 July 1423 which allowed a certain Déodat Bruguières to graze his cattle on the land of Clermont-Lodève in return for an annual fee of two setiers of oats[4]. On 27 July 1424, he was a witne
2 CONC ss, at the Château de Roquecourbe, to the marriage contract between Bernard d'Armagnac, Count of Pardiac, Viscount of Carlat and Murat and Éléonore de Bourbon, only daughter of Jacques de Bourbon and his first wife Béatrice d'Évre
2 CONC ux-Navarre[4],[9].
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 20 October 1424 and 1 November 1424, Tristan de Clermont was in Venice where he asked for permission to charter a galley to take him to Lecce [10].
2 CONT
2 CONT On September 30, 1428, at the castle of Copertino, Tristan de Clermont, who probably planned to go to France, wrote a will that informs us about his descendants. This journey probably did not take place until after the death of hi
2 CONC s wife, on 24 November 1429. He soon planned to remarry, and on 26 February 1431 began negotiations to marry Louise de la Tour d'Auvergne, daughter of Bertrand de La Tour d'Auvergne[3].
2 CONT
2 CONT On 23 July 1431, he attended, as a witness, at the episcopal palace of Béziers the homage paid by Odet de Lomagne to Jean Ier de Foix for the barony of Angles in the county of Bigorre[11], and for the castle of Serignac. Jean de F
2 CONC oix was then governor of Languedoc on behalf of Charles VII. This ceremony was held as part of a meeting of the States of Languedoc[12].
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 1431 and 1436, Tristan de Clermont-Lodève and his son Raymond ceded the lordship of Clermont-Lodève and their other possessions in France to their daughter and sister Antoinette de Guilhem de Clermont-Lodève and her husban
2 CONC d Pons de Caylus, lord of Castelnau de Bretenoux[13], on condition that Pons de Caylus take up the name and coat of arms of the Clermont-Lodève family[14].
2 CONT
2 CONT Direct family and descendants
2 CONT The will that Tristan de Clermont-Lodève made on 30 September 1429 before the notary Giovanni de Rotigio, and in the presence of witnesses, some of whom were French, with the advice of a famous jurist, Francesco Ammirato, who had 
2 CONC come from Lecce, tells us about the composition of his family, which included six children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond, who is not yet of age and who is his universal legatee,
2 CONT Sancia, who was already married to François II des Baux, Duke of Andria,
2 CONT Margaret who would later marry Antonio Ventimiglia, Marquis of Geraci Siculo, and Grand Admiral of the Kingdom of Sicily,
2 CONT Isabella, queen consort of Naples, wife of King Ferdinand, with an immense posterity.
2 CONT [show]
2 CONT Family circle of Tristan de Clermont-Lodève
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT (it) Fernando Guida and Mario Spedicato, Tristano di Chiaromonte signore della contea di Copertino tra Salento e Francia, Panico (Galatina), 2009, 150 p. (ISBN 978-88-88156-85-9)
2 CONT Philippe Huppé, Les seigneurs de Clermont-Lodève : du palais carolingien à la cour napolitaine, IXe – XVe siècles - volume 1, Saint-Estève, Les presses littéraires (Saint-Estève), 2007, 279 p. (ISBN 978-2-35073-221-3)
2 CONT Philippe Huppé, Les seigneurs de Clermont-Lodève : du palais carolingien à la cour napolitaine, IXe – XVe siècles - volume 2, Saint-Estève, Les presses littéraires (Saint-Estève), 2007, 175 p. (ISBN 978-2-35073-285-5)
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tristan_of_Clermont
2 CONT
2 CONT Bartholomew "Tristan" de Clermont-Lodève (1380 – c. 1432), Count of Copertino, was a French-born knight who married Catherine Orsini del Balzo, youngest daughter of Mary of Enghien and Raimondo Orsini del Balzo, Prince of Taranto.
2 CONC [1] He was the father of Isabella of Clermont, Princess of Taranto, the first consort of King Ferdinand I of Naples.
2 CONT
2 CONT Documents and sources about Tristan, whose proper first name was Bartholomew, are scarce. Tristan de Clermont (Italian: Tristano di Chiaromonte) became Count of Cupertino by his wife's dowry.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1429 Tristan divided the inheritance between his children. They were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond de Clermont (Raimondello di Chiaromonte), died on 2 March 1443, leaving his inheritance to his sister Sancia;
2 CONT Sancia, who married Francesco del Balzo, Duke of Andria[2]
2 CONT Margherita, who married Antonio Ventimiglia, Marquess of Geraci, Grand Admiral;
2 CONT Antonia, alleged somewhere to have married Thomas Palaeologus, titular Despot of Morea, as brother of Emperor Constantine and heir presumptive to the throne of the Byzantine Empire;
2 CONT Isabella, who married Ferdinand of Aragon, later King of Naples[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT https://ancestors.familysearch.org/en/GKDB-CDR/tristan-bartholomé-de-clermont-lodève-dì-chiaromonte-1380-1441
2 CONT
2 CONT https://dbpedia.org/page/Tristan_of_Clermont
