1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1403
1 DEAT
2 DATE MAY 1457
2 ADDR
3 CITY Milan
3 STAE Lombardy
3 CTRY Italy
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Less famous than the younger brother Giacomo, Liberio - who was born in the first twenty years of the century. XV - he had in fact made known soon for his talents of genius and man of government, and for his deep c
2 CONC ulture: a lover of letters, cultivated the friendship of poets and humanists like the anconitano Ciriaco Pizzicolli - the "pater antiquitatum", from which he was called "optimus Pieridum cultor" - and the Tolentinate Francesco Fil
2 CONC elfo, who, with his son Giovan Mario, was with him in epistolary relationships. Due to his singular political and military abilities, the young Liberio was soon entrusted with important assignments both in Ancona and in other Ital
2 CONC ian cities. In 1444 he played a decisive role in the talks that ended with the stipulation of peace agreements between Pope Eugene IV and the Count of Pavia, Francesco Sforza, to whom the pontiff recognized the possession of the M
2 CONC arca, but as a papal fief (10 October). Neither Sforza, who became the Duke of Milan, failed to tangibly witness to him his gratitude; just as he did not fail to benefit from his intelligent and precious work. In 1450 he appointed
2 CONC  him ducal commissioner in Alessandria and Tortona; in 1456, after Liberio had fulfilled his duties as ambassador of the Community of Ancona to Rome for peace negotiations with Venice, he wanted him as a mayor in Milan. However, t
2 CONC he Anconite was not to see the end of the important task: in May 1457, in the midst of his mandate, Liberio, struck by a plague, suddenly died out in Milan.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had married a noblewoman, Countess of the Trinci of Foligno, from whom he had a son, also called Liberio, born after his death. In 1448 he had obtained for himself and his brother Giacomo from Cardinal Prospero Colonna, nephew 
2 CONC of Martino V, the confirmation of the privileges granted to his father by that pope.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources and Bibles .: Ancona, Archivio Stor. Com., Acts of the Council of the Community of Ancona, years 1431-1439; Corpus actorum etdecretorum magniConstantiensis concilii, IV, Francofurti et Lipsiae 1699, p. 147; Oddo di Biagio,
2 CONC  Cronica de la edificationeetdestructione del Cassero, edited by C. Ciavarini, in Collez. of docc.stor. ancient ineditied rare editions of the cities and lands of the Marche, I, Ancona 1870, p. 123; Acta concilii Constanciensis, e
2 CONC dited by H. Funke, Monasterii 1896, p. 263; Ancona, Biblioteca Com., C. Albertini, Patres patriae, Catal. of the ancient and ancient families in the City of Ancona, pp. 49, 52; C. Santoro, The official Sforza domain, Milan 1950, p
2 CONC p. 139 and 527; G. Saracini, Not. historiche d'Ancona, Rome 1675, pp. 496 s .; G. Colucci, Picene Antiquity. Diz. of the illustrious men of Ancona, XXVII, Fermo 1796, pp. 27-29; A. Peruzzi, History of Ancona, II, Pesaro 1835, pp. 
2 CONC 321 s .; M. Morici, Dante and Ciriaco d'Ancona ..., Florence 1889, passim; M. Natalucci, Ancona through the centuries, I, Città di Castello 1961, pp. 364 ss., 391 ss., 422; V. Spreti, Encyclopedia stor-nob. Italian, II, pp. 116 s.
2 CONT
2 CONT From: http://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/pietro-bonarelli_(Dizionario-Biografico)/
