1 NOTE DUKE OF BEDFORD; KG; REGENT OF FRANCE; DUKE OF ANDEGAVIA & ALANSON; EARL OF
2 CONT CENOMANNIA, HARECOURT, & DREUX; VISCOUNT BEAUMONT; 12TH EARL OF RICHMOND
1 NOTE John of Lancaster, Duke of Bedford (1389-1435), 3d son of Henry IV by his first wife, Mary of Bohun. He was created Duke of Bedford (1414) by his brother, Henry V. After Henry�s death, in 1422, he became regent of England; and in 
2 CONC the struggle for the French crown which followed the death of Charles VI, he commanded the English army in France, proclaimed Henry VI a child of nine months, at Paris, and defeated the French at Verneuil (Aug. 17, 1424). [World W
2 CONC ide Illustrated Encyclopedia, 1935]
2 CONT
2 CONT Regent of France and Protector of England 1422-35. Died without issue. {Burke�s Peerage} [GADD.GED]
1 NOTE He was an English soldier and statesman. John was made lieutenant of 
2 CONT England when his brother invaded France in 1415, during the Hundred Years' 
2 CONT War. In 1416 John commanded the English fleet that defeated the French at 
2 CONT the mouth of the Seine River. In 1422, after his brother's death, John was 
2 CONT designated protector and defender of the kingdom for the young King Henry 
2 CONT VI. Transferring his duties in England to his brother Humphrey, duke of 
2 CONT Gloucester, John took over the conduct of English affairs in France in 
2 CONT order to consolidate the English position against further French 
2 CONT opposition. After reaffirming the alliance made in 1420 by Henry V with 
2 CONT Philip the Good, duke of Burgundy, John and Philip in 1424 gained a 
2 CONT victory at Verneuil, France, over the Dauphin, later King Charles VII of 
2 CONT France. In 1429 John's forces were defeated by the French heroine Joan of 
2 CONT Arc at the siege of Orléans and the subsequent Battle of Patay. Two years 
2 CONT later, when Joan, who had been captured by the Burgundians, was turned 
2 CONT over to the English, John allowed her to be burned at the stake as a 
2 CONT heretic. Thereafter John's plans for the conquest of France were 
2 CONT frustrated by the defection to France of Philip the Good.
