1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Antoine from Luxembourg from Wikipedia 
2 CONT
2 CONT Antoine I of Luxembourg (- † 1519) was count of Brienne , of Ligny (-en-Barrois) and of Roussy [ a 1 ] , baron of Rameru and of Piney , viscount of Machault [ b 1 ] (cf Enghien ) . He was first lieutenant general in Burgundy [ c 1
2 CONC  ] . Nevertheless, after having been a prisoner in France, he became a servant of the King of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT This section is empty, insufficiently detailed or incomplete. Your help is welcome! How to do ? He was the younger son [ 1 ] of Louis de Luxembourg (1418 - † 1475) and Jeanne de Bar (1415 - † 1462).
2 CONT
2 CONT Wedding He married in 1472 Antoinette de Bauffremont , countess of Charny and Montfort , heiress daughter of Pierre de Bauffremont [ b 1 ]
2 CONT
2 CONT Antoinette gave birth to Philiberte de Luxembourg , countess of Charny (- † 1539) [ b 1 ] , who will marry Jean IV de Chalon-Arlay (1443-1502) prince of Orange  : parents of Philibert (1502-1530) and Claude de Chalon , and grandpa
2 CONC rents of René de Chalon-Nassau (1519-1544); Second wife, he married Françoise de Croy - Chimay , daughter of Count Philippe I st of Croÿ-Chimay [ b 1 ] , [ 2 ]  :
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles I (1488-1530) count of Brienne , Ligny and Roussy , wife in 1510 Charlotte d'Estouteville , daughter of Jacques d'Estouteville, † 1509, lord of Beynes and Blainville , baron of Ivry-la-Chaussée , provost of Paris , and Gil
2 CONC lette de Coëtivy , granddaughter of Charles VII and last wife of Antoine de Luxembourg, see below):
2 CONT
2 CONT Antoine II († 1557) married Marguerite de Savoie, daughter of René :
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean IV (1537- † 1576) count of Brienne, Ligny, Roussy, wife Guillemette de La Marck daughter of Robert IV duc de Bouillon and count de Braine , hence the following of the counts of Brienne and Ligny of the Maisons de Luxembourg t
2 CONC hen of Béon and Loménie, by his son Charles II (1562-1608; without posterity from his wife Anne-Marie de Nogaret de Lavalette , married in 1583), and his daughter Louise de Brienne (1567-1647; x 1 ° Georges d'Amboise d'Aubijoux , 
2 CONC and 2 ° Bernard de Béon du Massès (c. 1548 / 1554-1607): parents of Louise de Béon (1585-1665), wife in 1623 of Henri-Auguste de Loménie  ; Ligny and Roussy passed in 1608 to uncle François de Piney, below);
2 CONT
2 CONT François (1542-1613), first duke of Piney-Luxembourg and prince of Tingry , husband of Diane de Lorraine, daughter of Claude II d'Aumale and Louise de Brézé (daughter of Diane de Poitiers and great-granddaughter of Charles VII ): 
2 CONC where Henry 2 e Duke of Piney-Luxembourg strain female succession of the House of Montmorency-Bouteville-Luxembourg  ;
2 CONT
2 CONT Madeleine married Christophe de Jouvenel des Ursins marquis de Traînel  ;
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis de Luxembourg († 1571), count of Roussy, third husband of Antoinette de Chaumont d'Amboise (1495-1552) lady of Chaumont , Lignières , Ravel , Meillant and Charenton etc., daughter of Guy de Chaumont and niece of Charles II o
2 CONC f Amboise  ;
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean de Luxembourg (c. 1515-1547 / 1548), bishop of Pamiers in 1539-1548, abbot of Ivry and Larrivour , poet [ 3 ]  ; Gillette / Guillemette, x 1532 François de Vienne sire d'Antigny , Commarin , Ruffey and Chevreaux  ;
2 CONT
2 CONT Françoise de Luxembourg (1520-1566) lady of Roussy , wife 1 ° Bernard III or IV margrave of Bade-Bade , then 2 ° Adolphe IV of Nassau - Idstein , son of Philippe , hence the posterity of the two marriages; Antoinette Abbess of ND 
2 CONC d'Yerres  ; and Marie Abbess of ND de Troyes . Settling in France, he married again Gillette de Coëtivy (- † 1510), widow in 1509 of Jacques d'Estouteville above, daughter of Olivier de Coëtivy (1418 - † 1480), count of Taillebour
2 CONC g and seneschal of Guyenne [ b 1 ] and Marie de Valois , illegitimate daughter of King Charles VII and Agnès Sorel .
2 CONT
2 CONT He had natural children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Antoine († 1538; son of Péronne de Machefert) married Isabelle de Marolles, hence the lords of Luxémont and La Chapelle-en-Brie  ; Isabeau married in 1489 Pierre Morlet de La Chaussée viscount d'Eu , hence the continuation of La C
2 CONC haussée d'Eu; Olivier ; Claude; Marie marries in 1495 Jacques bastard of Chalon (son of Jean IV prince of Orange above); Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Lieutenant General in Burgundy Under the orders of his father, in 1465 he campaigned for the League of Public Good against the King of France Louis XI [ a 2 ] .
2 CONT
2 CONT At the wedding of Charles the Bold and Margaret of York inJuly 1468, he fought in the tournament given in Bruges [ a 3 ] .
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1472, he was appointed lieutenant general in Burgundy by the duke [ c 1 ] . During this summer of 1472, he ravaged the border countries between Champagne and Burgundy, in particular the territory of the county of Tonnerre [ a 4
2 CONC  ] .
2 CONT
2 CONT The June 20, 1475, he was taken during the battle of Guipy near Château-Chinon [ a 5 ] , [ c 2 ] and was locked in the tower of Bourges , then brought to King Louis XI , at the Château du Plessis-du-Parc -léz-Tours . Louis XI heav
2 CONC ily demanded from him a ransom of 40,000 crowns [ a 6 ] , [ c 3 ] . TheSeptember 13, 1475, he was appointed as one of the curators of the Soleuvres truce [ a 7 ] .
2 CONT
2 CONT “Mess rs , you are all master Jehan, and you are better off at Franchise than at Guysnes in hostaige of Mons r de Roussy. ......... Escript the Forges, the X th day of November [1480]. LOYS. D E D OYAT . A noz amez et feaulx advis
2 CONC ers and chamberlains the lords of Baudricourt, of Bouchaige and Soliers (ambassadors of the king) [ a 8 ] . "
2 CONT
2 CONT End of Constable Louis of Luxembourg Due to the geographical and political situation, Louis de Luxembourg-Saint-Pol , Antoine's father, had been playing a double game between two powerful princes since 1465 despite his status as c
2 CONC onstable of France [ c 4 ] . Already in May 1474 , the French and Burgundian envoys concluded that Louis de Luxembourg, a criminal enemy, should be put to death. The Peace of Picquigny , a treaty signed on August 29, 1475, was fin
2 CONC al. Because, King Edward IV of England sent Louis XI letters from Saint-Pol in order to aggravate the war. Finally, even Duke Charles the Bold decided to have Louis of Luxembourg arrested [c 5 ] . On November 26, 1475, Saint-Pol w
2 CONC as sent to Paris. As soon as the Parliament of Paris condemned him to death on December 19 after three weeks of trial, his head was severed the same day [ c 6 ] . As a result, the Duke of Burgundy received Saint-Quentin , Ham and 
2 CONC Bohain to the detriment of the Luxembourg family. [What?]
2 CONT
2 CONT In the service of the King of France However, he was appointed by Louis XII ordinary chamberlain, and often in charge of important negotiations. Above all, the king restored him to his property, by letters dated from Blois leMay 2
2 CONC 9, 1504[ b 1 ] .
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1510, following the death of Charles de Bourbon without a male heir, the Comte de Ligny was returned to Antoine.
