1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Helena_Dragas
2 CONT
2 CONT Helena Dragases (Serbian: Јелена Драгаш / Jelena Dragaš) (c. 1372 – 23 May 1450) was the Empress consort of Manuel II Palaiologos/
2 CONT
2 CONT  Family
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a daughter of Constantine Dragaš, a regional semi-independent lord in the fragmenting Serbian realm centered at Velbăžd (Kyustendil).
2 CONT
2 CONT Her mother was his unnamed first wife, not his second wife Eudokia Megaskomnene. Her stepmother was a daughter of Alexios III of Trebizond and Theodora Kantakouzene.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father died in 1395 at the batlle of Rovine. Constantine Dragaš was killed while fighting for his overlord Sultan Bayezid I of the Ottoman Empire against the rebel Mircea I of Wallachia.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT On 10 February 1392, Helena married Manuel II Palaiologos, Byzantine Emperor. They had several children. The list follows the orders of births given by George Sphrantzes:
2 CONT
2 CONT *A daughter. Mentioned as the eldest daughter but not named. Possibly confused with Isabella Palaiologina, an illegitimate daughter of Manuel II known to have married Ilario Doria. 
2 CONT
2 CONT *Constantine Palaiologos. Died young. 
2 CONT
2 CONT *John VIII Palaiologos (18 December 1392 - 31 October 1448). Byzantine emperor 1425-1448. 
2 CONT
2 CONT *Andronikos Palaiologos, Lord of Thessalonike (d. 1429). 
2 CONT
2 CONT *A second daughter. Also not named in the text. 
2 CONT
2 CONT *Theodore II Palaiologos, Lord of Morea (d. 1448). 
2 CONT
2 CONT *Michael Palaiologos. Died young. 
2 CONT
2 CONT *Constantine XI (8 February 1405 – 29 May 1453). Byzantine emperor 1448–1453. 
2 CONT
2 CONT *Demetrios Palaiologos (c. 1407–1470). Despotēs in Morea. 
2 CONT
2 CONT *Thomas Palaiologos (c. 1409 – 12 May 1465). Despotēs in Morea. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Manuel II died on 21 July 1425. Helena survived him by almost twenty five years. She retired to a monastery under the monastic name Hypomone (Patience). Helena died on May 23, 1450 in Constantinople.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her children included the last two Byzantine emperors. Constantine XI renamed himself after her maiden name Dragaš (in Greek, Dragasēs), adding it to his own.
