1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andronikos_III_Palaiologos
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Andronikos III Palaiologos''' (Medieval Greek: Ἀνδρόνικος Δούκας Ἄγγελος Κομνηνός Παλαιολόγος, romanized: Andrónikos Doúkās Ángelos Komnēnós Palaiológos; 25 March 1297 – 15 June 1341), commonly Latinized as '''Andronicus III Pa
2 CONC laeologus''', was the Byzantine emperor from 1328 to 1341.[1] He was the son of Michael IX Palaiologos and Rita of Armenia. He was proclaimed co-emperor in his youth, before 1313, and in April 1321 he rebelled in opposition to his
2 CONC  grandfather, Andronikos II Palaiologos. He was formally crowned co-emperor in February 1325, before ousting his grandfather outright and becoming sole emperor on 24 May 1328.
2 CONT
2 CONT His reign included the last failed attempts to hold back the Ottoman Turks in Bithynia and the defeat at Rusokastro against the Bulgarians, but also the successful recovery of Chios, Lesbos, Phocaea, Thessaly, and Epirus. His earl
2 CONC y death left a power vacuum that resulted in the disastrous civil war between his widow, Anna of Savoy, and his closest friend and supporter, John VI Kantakouzenos, leading to the establishment of the Serbian Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Contents
2 CONT #	[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andronikos_III_Palaiologos#Life Life]
