1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sir John Grey, of Groby, Leicestershire (c. 1432[1] – 17 February 1461) was a Lancastrian knight, the first husband of Elizabeth Woodville who later married King Edward IV of England, and great-great-grandfather of
2 CONC  Lady Jane Grey.  Sir John Grey was killed in the Second Battle of St Albans in 1461, fighting for the Lancastrian cause.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family==
2 CONT
2 CONT Grey was the son and heir of Elizabeth Ferrers, 6th Baroness Ferrers of Groby (1419–1483) and of Sir Edward Grey (c. 1415–1457).[1][2] His father was summoned to parliament as Baron Ferrers of Groby in right of his wife.[1][3] Aft
2 CONC er the death of Grey's father his stepfather, John Bourchier, assumed his wife's title, Baron Ferrers of Groby.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Married 
2 CONT # Abt 8 Jan 1454-1455  [3, 17] to Elizabeth Wydeville (b. 1437, Grafton Regis, Northamptonshire, England ,   d. 8 Jun 1492, Bermondsey Abbey, Surrey, England).  Her 1st husband.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Children 	
2 CONT *  	1. Sir Thomas Grey, 1st Marquess of Dorset,   b. Cal 1455, of, Groby, Leicestershire, England ,   d. 20 Sep 1501, Astley, Warwickshire, England.    Married 1) Anne de Holand 2) Cecily Bonville
2 CONT *  	2. Sir Richard Grey, Knight,   b. Abt 1459, of, Groby, Leicestershire, England ,   d. 13 Jun 1483, Pomfret, Yorkshire, England.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations 
2 CONT * 1. Douglas Richardson. Plantagenet Ancestry: A Study In Colonial And Medieval Families, 2nd Edition, 2011. pg 161-164.
2 CONT * 2.  Sir John Grey (c.1432-1461), knight in Grey, Sir Richard (d. 1483), nobleman by Rosemary Horrox in Dictionary of National Biography online (accessed 26 November 2007)
2 CONT * 3. [S2221] #244 The History and Antiquities of the County of Northampton (1822-1841), Baker, George, (2 volumes. London: J. B. Nichols and Son, 1822-1841), FHL book Q 942.55 H2bal; FHL microfilm 962,237 ite., vol. 1 p. 56.
2 CONT * 17.  [S20] Magna Carta Ancestry: A study in Colonial and Medieval Families, Richardson, Douglas, (Kimball G. Everingham, editor. 2nd edition, 2011), vol. 2 p. 303.
2 CONT
