2 CONT *'''George Grey, 2nd Earl of Kent1
2 CONT *'''M, #120637, d. 21 December 1503
2 CONT *Last Edited=1 May 2011
2 CONT *'''     George Grey, 2nd Earl of Kent was the son of Edmund Grey, 1st Earl of Kent and Lady Catherine Percy.2,3 He married, firstly, Lady Anne Woodville, daughter of Richard Wydevill, 1st Earl Rivers and Jacquetta de Luxembourg, 
2 CONC after 1483.4 He married, secondly, Lady Catherine Herbert, daughter of William Herbert, 1st Earl of Pembroke and Anne Devereux, before 1 October 1490.5 He died on 21 December 1503.4 He was also reported to have died on 16 December
2 CONC  1503.6
2 CONT *'''     He held the office of Justice of the Peace (J.P.) for Huntingdonshire in 1480.6 He held the office of Justice of the Peace (J.P.) for Northamptonshire in 1480.6 He was invested as a Knight, Order of the Bath (K.B.) in 148
2 CONC 3.6 He held the office of Justice of the Peace (J.P.) for Bedfordshire in 1483.6 He fought in the Battle of Stoke in 1487, for King Henry VII.6 He succeeded to the title of 2nd Earl of Kent [E., 1465] on 22 May 1490.3 He succeeded
2 CONC  to the title of 5th Lord Grey, of Ruthin [E., 1325] on 22 May 1490.3 He gained the rank of Commander in 1491 in the service of the Emperor Maximilian's forces in France.3 He held the office of Justice of the Peace (J.P.) for Buck
2 CONC inghamshire in 1494.6 He held the office of Justice of the Peace (J.P.) for Kent in 1496.6 He fought in the Battle of Blackheath in 1497, against the rebels.6 He was Commander of the forces against the Cornishman uprising.3
2 CONT *'''Child of George Grey, 2nd Earl of Kent and Lady Anne Woodville
2 CONT ** 1.Richard Grey, 3rd Earl of Kent7 b. 1481, d. 3 May 1523
2 CONT *'''Children of George Grey, 2nd Earl of Kent and Lady Catherine Herbert
2 CONT ** 1.Lady Anne Grey+1 d. fr 1 Mar 1544/45 - 11 Feb 1545/46
2 CONT ** 2.George Grey3
2 CONT ** 3.Anthony Grey+3
2 CONT ** 4.Tacy Grey+6
2 CONT ** 5.Henry Grey, 4th Earl of Kent+5 b. a 1482, d. 24 Sep 1562
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT * 1.[S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Exti
2 CONC nct or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume II, page 76. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT * 2.[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume VI, page 160.
2 CONT * 3.[S21] L. G. Pine, The New Extinct Peerage 1884-1971: Containing Extinct, Abeyant, Dormant and Suspended Peerages With Genealogies and Arms (London, U.K.: Heraldry Today, 1972), page 137. Hereinafter cited as The New Extinct Pe
2 CONC erage.
2 CONT * 4.[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume II, page 249.
2 CONT * 5.[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume VII, page 167.
2 CONT * 6.[S37] BP2003 volume 2, page 1666. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT * 7.[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume VII, page 168.
2 CONT *From: http://www.thepeerage.com/p12064.htm#i120637
2 CONT *_____________________________
2 CONT *'''George GREY (2° E. Kent)
2 CONT *'''Born: BEF 1454, Ruthin, Denbighshire, Wales
2 CONT *'''Acceded: 1490
2 CONT *'''Died: 16/25 Dec 1503, Ampthill, Bedfordshire, England
2 CONT *'''Notes: 5° B. Ruthyn. Cokayne, The Complete Peerage vol.VII,pp.166-167. "Complete Peerage", 2° ed., vol. VII, pg. 160. George was his parents' second son. His older brother, Anthony, did not survive his father. George was sent 
2 CONC into France as a commander in 1491, to assist the Emperor Maximilian, and was Chief Commander against the Cornishmen in their rising, under Lord Audley. L.G. Pine, "The New Extinct Peerage 1884-1971" (1973), Genealogical Publishin
2 CONC g Co., Inc. Baltimore, pg. 137. Burke, "Genealogical and Heraldic History of the Peerage Baronatage and Knightage", 97th ed:, (1826), pg.1154.
2 CONT *Father: Edmund GREY (1° E. Kent)
2 CONT *Mother: Catherine PERCY (C. Kent)
2 CONT *'''Married 1: Anne WOODVILLE (V. Bourchier/C. Kent) Sat 26 Jun 1480
2 CONT *Children:
2 CONT **1. Richard GREY (3º E. Kent)
2 CONT *'''Married 2: Catherine HERBERT (C. Kent) 10 Jan 1489/90/ 1 Oct 1490
2 CONT *Children:
2 CONT **2. Henry GREY (4º E. Kent)
2 CONT **3. Anthony GREY of Brancepeth (Hon.)
2 CONT **4. Anne GREY (B. Hussey of Old Sleaford)
2 CONT *From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/GREY3.htm#George GREY (2° E. Kent)
2 CONT *_________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT George Grey, 2nd Earl of Kent held the office of Justice of the Peace (J.P.) for Huntingdonshire in 1480.
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Justice of the Peace (J.P.) for Northamptonshire in 1480.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was invested as a Knight, Order of the Bath (K.B.) in 1483.
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Justice of the Peace (J.P.) for Bedfordshire in 1483.
2 CONT
2 CONT He fought in the Battle of Stoke in 1487, for King Henry VII.
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded to the title of 5th Lord Grey, of Ruthin [E., 1325] on 22 May 1490.
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded to the title of 2nd Earl of Kent [E., 1465] on 22 May 1490.
2 CONT
2 CONT He gained the rank of Commander in 1491 in the service of the Emperor Maximilian's forces in France.
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Justice of the Peace (J.P.) for Buckinghamshire in 1494.
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Justice of the Peace (J.P.) for Kent in 1496.
2 CONT
2 CONT He fought in the Battle of Blackheath in 1497, against the rebels.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was Commander of the forces against the Cornishman uprising.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ruthin Castle is a medieval castle fortification in Wales, near the town of Ruthin in the Vale of Clwyd. ￼
2 CONT It was constructed during the late 13th century by Dafydd, the brother of Prince Llywelyn II, on a red sandstone ridge overlooking the valley. The castle was most notably the base of that branch of the noble de Grey family accorde
2 CONC d the title "Barons Grey de Ruthyn" and the head of their marcher lordship of Dyffryn Clwyd. It was a base for Reginald Grey, 3rd Baron Grey de Ruthyn – the man who could be said to have sparked the rebellion of Owain Glyndŵr.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the English Civil War Oliver Cromwell's forces dismantled and demolished the castle, in a process of organised defortification called slighting, to prevent the castle's becoming militarily viable in the future. In 1923 the 
2 CONC castle became Britain's first private hospital for the investigation and treatment of obscure internal diseases. Since the 1960s the castle remains have been incorporated into a hotel
2 CONT *________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Below information was taken from Wkipedia.  George Grey, 2nd Earl of Kent and 5th Baron Grey de Ruthyn, was the second son of the 1st Earl, his elder brother having died in 1480. He was made a Knight of the Bath by King Richard II
2 CONC I in July 1483. He was a Justice of the Peace for Huntingdonshire from 1480, for Northamptonshire from 1480 and for Bedfordshire from 1483, for Buckinghamshire from 1494 and for Kent from 1496. Grey fought for Henry VII against th
2 CONC e Yorkist pretender Perkin Warbeck at the Battle of Stoke Field on 16 June 1487. In 1491 he was a commissioner to raise money in Bedfordshire for a war against France. On 17 June 1497, eleven years and one day after Stoke Field he
2 CONC  again fought in a royal army when it defeated Cornish rebels at the Battle of Deptford Bridge (also known as the Battle of Blackheath). Grey died at Ampthill in December 1505. Earlier that year he had attended court on the visit 
2 CONC of King Philip I of Castile (1504–1506) A letter from William Makefyr to Robert D'arcy and Giles Alington of 17 January 1506 reads [CRT190/45]: "The Earl of Kent was mounted on a sorrelled horse, harness of Venice gold with a deep
2 CONC  fringe half a yard in length. My Lord of Kent's Coat was one bar of Gold Cloth and another crimson Velvet with a demi-mache cut off by the elbow…a true soldier too, and a favourite of Henry but survived this pageant a very short 
2 CONC time, dying within the year" www.findagrave.com
2 CONT
2 CONT George Grey
2 CONT BIRTH	1454
2 CONT DEATH	25 Dec 1505 (aged 50–51)
2 CONT Ampthill, Central Bedfordshire Unitary Authority, Bedfordshire, England
2 CONT BURIAL	
2 CONT Wardon Abbey
2 CONT Old Warden, Central Bedfordshire Unitary Authority, Bedfordshire, England
2 CONT MEMORIAL ID	201775316 
