2 CONT *''''''Joan STRANGE (10° B. Strange of Knockin)
2 CONT *''''''Born: ABT 1463
2 CONT *''''''Died: 20 Mar 1513/14, Colham Green, Midlessex, London, England
2 CONT *Father: John Le STRANGE (9º B. Strange of Knockin)
2 CONT *Mother: Jacquetta WOODVILLE (B. Strange of Knockyn)
2 CONT *''''''Married: George STANLEY (B. Strange of Knockin) BEF 26 Feb 1481/82 
2 CONT *Children:
2 CONT **1. John STANLEY 
2 CONT **2. Thomas STANLEY (2° E. Derby)
2 CONT **3. James STANLEY of Crosshall (Esq. Marshall of Ireland) 
2 CONT **4. Mary STANLEY 
2 CONT **5. Jane STANLEY 
2 CONT **6. Elizabeth STANLEY 
2 CONT **7. Eleanor STANLEY 
2 CONT *From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/STRANGE.htm#Joan STRANGE (10° B. Strange of Knockin)
2 CONT *_______________________
2 CONT '''George Stanley, 9th Baron Strange''', of Knockyn, KG, KB (1460–1503) was an English nobleman and heir of Thomas Stanley, 1st Earl of Derby. Despite predeceasing his father in 1503, he was nevertheless a considerable soldier and
2 CONC  aristocrat in his own right and held a number of senior offices of state.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born about 1460 in Knowsley, Lancashire, England, the eldest son of Thomas Stanley and his first wife Eleanor, sister of Richard Neville, Earl of Warwick. On his father's second marriage to Lady Margaret Beaufort, Countess 
2 CONC of Richmond he became stepbrother to her son, Henry Tudor, later King Henry VII.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was invested as a Knight of the Order of the Bath in 1475 by King Edward IV. He held the offices of Constable of Pontefract Castle and Constable of Knaresborough Castle in 1485. He was present at the battle of Bosworth Field, b
2 CONC ut was held captive by Richard III because of his father's actions. He held the office of Constable of Wicklow Castle in 1486 and became Chief Justice of the Duchy of Lancaster in 1486. In 1487 he was invested with the Order of th
2 CONC e Garter and made a Privy Counsellor.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''He was married in 1482 to Joan le Strange, 9th Baroness Strange (c. 1460 - 1514)''', daughter of John le Strange, 8th Baron Strange (c. 1440 - 1477) and Jacquetta Woodville (daughter of Richard Woodville, 1st Earl Rivers and Ja
2 CONC cquetta of Luxembourg), '''thereby becoming 9th Baron Strange de jure uxoris (in the right of his wife)''' and was summoned to Parliament in this role. '''Their children were:'''
2 CONT * John Stanley (d. 1503)
2 CONT * Thomas Stanley, 2nd Earl of Derby (1485–1521)
2 CONT * James Stanley, Knight (1486–1562), who founded the branch of the family known as the 'Stanleys of Bickerstaffe', from which the 11th Earl of Derby and all subsequent Earls descend
2 CONT * George Stanley
2 CONT * Jane Stanley, married Sir Robert Sheffield (died 15 November 1531, son of Robert Sheffield and Helen Delves) and had a son, Edmund Sheffield, 1st Baron Sheffield.
2 CONT * Elizabeth Stanley
2 CONT * Margaret Stanley
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in Derby House, St Paul's Wharf, London over 4–5 December 1503, allegedly of poison following a banquet. He was buried in the London church of St James Garlickhythe nearby.
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Stanley,_9th_Baron_Strange
2 CONT *___________________________
2 CONT Edmund Sheffield, 1st Baron Sheffield, of Butterwick (22 November 1521 – 19 July 1549) was an English nobleman, the son of Sir Robert Sheffield (died 15 November 1531, son of Robert Sheffield and Helen Delves) and his second wife 
2 CONC Jane Stanley, '''daughter of George Stanley, 9th Baron Strange and Joan le Strange, 9th Baroness Strange'''. Through his mother, he was a second cousin once removed of the reigning English monarch, King Henry VIII.
2 CONT Life
2 CONT Following his father's death in 1531, his wardship was granted to George Boleyn, Viscount Rochford, the brother of Henry VIII's second wife, Anne Boleyn. However, both Rochford and Boleyn were executed in 1536, and his wardship wa
2 CONC s transferred to John de Vere, the fifteenth Earl of Oxford.
2 CONT Sheffield married Anne de Vere, daughter of John de Vere, 15th Earl of Oxford, before 31 January 1538, and by her had a son and three daughters. In 1547 he was raised to the Peerage of England as Baron Sheffield of Butterwick. Two
2 CONC  years later, during Kett's Rebellion in Norwich, Lord Sheffield was killed in a street near the Cathedral Close when he fell from his horse, removed his helmet assuming the customary capture and eventual ransom, yet was fatally s
2 CONC truck by a butcher, reputedly named Fulke.[1] [2] With the loss of a senior commander and his army being broken up in street fighting, Northampton ordered a retreat.[3]
2 CONT He was buried at St Martin at Palace, Norwich.
2 CONT Children
2 CONT 1.Eleanor Sheffield (born about 1537) married Denzel Holles
2 CONT 2.John Sheffield, 2nd Baron Sheffield (c. 1538 – 10 December 1568) married Douglas Howard
2 CONT 3.Robert Sheffield (born about 1540)
2 CONT 4.Frances Sheffield (born 1542) married Thomas Metham
2 CONT 5.Elizabeth Sheffield (born about 1546)
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edmund_Sheffield,_1st_Baron_Sheffield
