0 @N6000000002188684131@ NOTE Guy de Montfort, Count of Nola
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT Guy de Montfort, Count of Nola (1244 – c. 1288) was the son of Simon de Montfort, 6th Earl of Leicester and Eleanor of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT He participated in the Battle of Evesham against the royalist forces of his uncle, King Henry III of England, and his cousin, Prince Edward. Both his father and his elder brother were killed during the disastrous battle, and Guy w
1 CONC as seriously wounded and taken prisoner.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was held at Windsor Castle until spring 1266, when he bribed his captors and escaped to France to rejoin his exiled family. Guy and his brother, Simon the younger, wandered across Europe for several years, eventually making the
1 CONC ir way to Italy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Guy took service with Charles of Anjou, serving as his Vicar-General in Tuscany. There he married an Italian noblewoman, Margherita Aldobrandeschi, and had two daughters:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Anastasia, married Romano Orsini
1 CONT    2. Tomasina, married Pietro Vico
1 CONT
1 CONT He distinguished himself at the Battle of Alba and was given Nola by Charles of Anjou.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1271 Guy and Simon discovered their cousin Henry of Almain (son of Richard, Earl of Cornwall) was in Viterbo at the church of San Silvestro. In revenge for the deaths of their father and brother at Evesham, Guy and Simon murder
1 CONC ed Henry while he clutched the altar, begging for mercy. "You had no mercy for my father and brothers", was Guy's reply. For this crime the Montfort brothers were excommunicated, and Dante banished Guy to the river of boiling bloo
1 CONC d in the seventh circle of his Inferno (Canto XII).
1 CONT
1 CONT Simon died later that year at Siena, "cursed by God, a wanderer and a fugitive". Guy was stripped of his titles and took service with Charles of Anjou again, but was captured in 1287 by the Aragonese at the Battle of the Counts. H
1 CONC e died in prison.
1 CONT
1 CONT Among his direct descendants (via his elder daughter, Anastasia): late 15th century Kings of Naples, England's Queen-Consort Elizabeth Woodville, 16th century rulers of Poland, Dukes of Ferrera, and Dukes of Guise.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Maddicott, J.R. Simon de Montfort, 1996
0 @N6000000002909004255@ NOTE Guy de Montfort, Count of Nola (1244 – c. 1288) was the son of Simon de Montfort, 6th Earl of Leicester and Eleanor of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT He participated in the Battle of Evesham against the royalist forces of his uncle, King Henry III of England, and his cousin, Prince Edward. Both his father and his elder brother were killed during the disastrous battle, and Guy w
1 CONC as seriously wounded and taken prisoner.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was held at Windsor Castle until spring 1266, when he bribed his captors and escaped to France to rejoin his exiled family. Guy and his brother, Simon the younger, wandered across Europe for several years, eventually making the
1 CONC ir way to Italy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Guy took service with Charles of Anjou, serving as his Vicar-General in Tuscany. There he married an Italian noblewoman, Margherita Aldobrandesca, the Lady of Sovana, heiress of a branch of the Aldobrandeschi family, with a feudal
1 CONC  contado, nominally subject to Orvieto, that stretched from the shores of the Tyrrhenian sea to the borders of Acquapendente. With her he had two daughters:[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT Anastasia, married Romano Orsini[2] 
1 CONT Tomasina, married Pietro Vico 
1 CONT He distinguished himself at the Battle of Alba and was given Nola by Charles of Anjou.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1271 Guy and Simon discovered their cousin Henry of Almain (son of Richard, Earl of Cornwall) was in Viterbo at the church of San Silvestro. In revenge for the deaths of their father and brother at Evesham, Guy and Simon murder
1 CONC ed Henry while he clutched the altar, begging for mercy. "You had no mercy for my father and brothers", was Guy's reply. For this crime the Montfort brothers were excommunicated, and Dante banished Guy to the river of boiling bloo
1 CONC d in the seventh circle of his Inferno (Canto XII).
1 CONT
1 CONT Simon died later that year at Siena, "cursed by God, a wanderer and a fugitive". Guy was stripped of his titles and took service with Charles of Anjou again, but was captured off the coast of Sicily in 1287 by the Aragonese at the
1 CONC  Battle of the Counts. He died in a Sicialian prison.
1 CONT
1 CONT Among his direct descendants (via his elder daughter, Anastasia): late 15th century Kings of Naples, England's Queen-Consort Elizabeth Woodville, 16th century rulers of Poland, Dukes of Ferrera, and Dukes of Guise.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ Margherita, ward of Benedetto Cardinal Caetani, survived him and went on to be married four times more. (G. Ciacchi, Gli Aldobrandeschi nella storia e nella 'Divina Commedia' , (Rome) 1935, vol. i, ch. 6). 
1 CONT ^ Napoleone Cardinal Orsini succeeded Benedetto Caetani as her mother's guardian, married her to his brother Orsello and arranged this marriage, which brought Sovana and the Aldobrandeschi inheritance to the Orsini. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Maddicott, J.R. Simon de Montfort, 1996 
0 @N6000000003658776281@ NOTE From Plantagenet Ancestry:
1 CONT
1 CONT GUY (or GUIDO) DE MONTFORT, Count of Nola, vicar-general in Tuscany, 4th son, born about 1243.  He and his brother, Henry, commanded the van of the Barons' Army at the Battel of Lewes 14 May 1264.  He was wounded and taken prisone
1 CONC r at the Battle of Evesham 4 August 1265.  He escaped from Dover Castle in April or May 1266, and afterwards went to France and thence to Italy.  He fought at Alba in 1268.  He married at Viterbo, Italy ... MARGHERITA ALDOBRANDESC
1 CONC HA, signorina of Grosseto, Soana, and Pitigliano, daughter and heiress of Ildebrandino Aldobrandeschi (styled "Count Rosso"). ... She was born about 1253-4.  They had two daughters. ... In 1271 he and his brother, Simon, murdered 
1 CONC their cousin, Henry of Almain ... in a church at Viterbo, Italy.  Guy was subsequently deprived of his lands and in 1273 he was excommunicated by Pope Gregory X.  In 1281 he was restored to favor with Charles, King of Sicily.  IN 
1 CONC 1283 Pope Martin IV pardoned him and alloweed him to claim his wife's inheritance.  The same year the Pope appointed him captain-general of the papal forces in Romagna.  In 1287, while in the service of Charles of Anjou, he headed
1 CONC  a fleet in the Bay of Naples for an attack upon rebellious Sicilians.  He was captured and subsequently imprisoned.  GUY DE MONTFORT, Count of Nola, died in prison in Sicily in 1291.  His widow, Margherita, married (2nd) Orsello 
1 CONC Orsini. ... They had two daughters, Angela ... and Maria. ... Margherita married (3rd) in 1303 Nello de' Pannocchieschi.
