0 @N6000000002188556228@ NOTE Richard Woodville, 1st Earl Rivers
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard Woodville (or Wydeville), 1st Earl Rivers (1405 – August 12, 1469), was an English nobleman, best remembered as the father of Elizabeth Woodville, wife of Edward IV.
1 CONT
1 CONT Born at Maidstone, Kent, he was the son of another Sir Richard Wydeville or Wydevill, chamberlain to the Duke of Bedford. After the duke died, the younger Richard married the widowed duchess, Jacquetta of Luxembourg (1416-1472). T
1 CONC his was initially a secret marriage for which the couple were fined when it came to public notice.
1 CONT
1 CONT Originally a mere squire from Grafton, Richard was considered "the handsomest man in England" and rose to become the squire of Henry V at the Battle of Agincourt in which the house of Lancaster conquered France. He managed to keep
1 CONC  the king's lucky totem of a squirrel's tail tied to a lance "always within sight of the king" during the battle, and was knighted afterwards.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was created Baron Rivers by Henry VI on May 9, 1448. Initially, he was on the Lancastrian side in the Wars of the Roses, but he later changed allegiances, and became a Yorkist, once he was convinced that the Lancastrian cause w
1 CONC as lost, and he reconciled himself to the new king, his future son-in-law. After the marriage of his daughter, Elizabeth Woodville, widow of Sir John Grey of Groby, to Edward IV on May 1, 1464, he was created Earl Rivers (1466), a
1 CONC nd appointed Lord Treasurer by his new son-in-law.
1 CONT
1 CONT The power of this new family was very distasteful to the old baronial party, and especially so to the Earl of Warwick. Early in 1468, the Riverses' estates were plundered by Warwick's partisans, and the open war of the following y
1 CONC ear was aimed to destroy the Woodvilles. After the king's defeat at the Battle of Edgecote Moor on 26 July 1469, Rivers and his second son, John, were taken prisoners at Chepstow. Following a hasty show trial, they were beheaded a
1 CONC t Kenilworth. His eldest son, Anthony succeeded him in the earldom.
1 CONT
1 CONT Lord Rivers had a large family. His third son, Lionel, (d. 1484), became the Bishop of Salisbury. All his daughters made great marriages: Catherine Woodville, the fifth child, was wife of Henry Stafford, 2nd Duke of Buckingham.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Children of Richard Woodville and Jacquetta of Luxembourg
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Elizabeth Woodville (1437 - 1492)
1 CONT     * Anthony Woodville, Lord Scales (1442 - June 25, 1483)
1 CONT     * Margaret Woodville married Lord Maltravers heir to the Earl of Arundel
1 CONT     * John Woodville (c. 1444 - August 12, 1469) married Catherine Neville, Dowager Duchess of Norfolk.
1 CONT     * Catherine Woodville (c. 1454 - c. 1509 married the Duke of Buckingham
1 CONT     * Anne Woodville married William Bourchier, Viscount Bourchier, heir to the Earl of Essex
1 CONT     * Eleanor Woodville married Anthony Grey, heir to the Earl of Kent
1 CONT     * Jacquetta Woodville married Lord Strange of Knokyn
1 CONT     * Mary Woodville married William Herbert, 2nd Earl of Pembroke
1 CONT     * Edward Woodville
1 CONT     * Lionel Woodville (c. 1446 - 1484)
1 CONT     * Richard Woodville
1 CONT     * John Woodville
1 CONT
