0 @N6000000003658827002@ NOTE From Wikipedia:
1 CONT
1 CONT Anthony Rivers, 2nd Earl Rivers (1442? - June 25, 1483) was an English nobleman, courtier, and writer.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the eldest son of Richard Woodville, 1st Earl Rivers and Jacquetta of Luxembourg. Like his father, he was originally a Lancastrian, fighting on that side at the Battle of Towton, but later became a Yorkist. He succeeded his
1 CONC  father in 1469.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rivers became very influential at the royal court after his sister Elizabeth married Edward IV. He joined the king in his temporary exile in 1470, and returned with him the next year, where he was wounded at the Battle of Barnet.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was married to Elizabeth de Scales, Baroness Scales in her own right, daughter of Thomas de Scales, 7th Baron Scales, and widow of Henry Bourchier, younger son of Henry Bourchier, 1st Earl of Essex.
1 CONT
1 CONT From Plantagenet Ancestry
1 CONT
1 CONT ANTHONY WYDEVILLE, Knt., K.G., 2nd Earl Rivers, 8th Lord Scales, of Grafton Regis, Northamptonshire, Chief Butler of England, Governor of the Prince of Wales, Constable of Portchester Castle, son and heir, born about 1442 (ages 26
1 CONC  in 1469, ages 30 in 1472).  He married (1st) probably before 8 April 1461 ELIZABETH SCALES, widow of Henry Bourghchier (or Bourchier), Knt. (probably died at Ludlow August (?) 1458), 2nd son of Henry Bourghchier, Knt., K.G., Earl
1 CONC  of Essex, and daughter and heiress of THomas Scales, Knt., 7th Lord Scales, Seneschal of Normandy, by Ismanie, daughter of John Whalesborough.  She was born about 1436 (aged 24 in 1460).  They had no issue.  He was summoned to Pa
1 CONC rliament 22 Dec. 1462 as Lord Scales.  the same year he accompanied the king to Scotland and took part in the siege of Alnwick.  He served as cupbearer at the coronation of his sister, Elizabeth, as Queen in 1465.  In 1466 he had 
1 CONC a grant of the Isle of Wight, with Carisbrooke Castle.  He fought with great valor at the Battle of Barnet in 1471, where he was wounded.  He was one of the earliest patrons of Caston, father of English printing.  His wife, Elizab
1 CONC eth, died 2 Sept. 1473.  In 1476 he was at the siege of Morat with the Duke of Burgundy.  He married (2nd) before Oct. 1480 Mary Fitz Lewis, daughter and heiress of Henry Fitz Lewis, Knt., of Bromsford (in Nevendon), Essex, by his
1 CONC  1st wife, Elizabeth, daughter of Edmund Beauford, K.G., Duke of Somerset.  They had no issue.  He had an illegitimate daughter, Margaret, by a mistress, Gwenllian Stradling, daughter of William Stradling, of ruthin, Denbighshire,
1 CONC  by Annes, daughter of John Gwyn.  In 1483, while en route to London, the Duke of Gloucester procured his arrest on a charge of attempting to usurp the government.  SIR ANTHONY WYDEVILLE, 2nd Earl Rivers, was beheaded without tria
1 CONC l at Pontefract, Yorkshire 25 June 1483.  His widow, Mary, married (2nd) george Neville, Knt., Esquire of the Body to King Richard III, illegitimate son of Thomas Neville, Knt.  They had issue.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1473 King Edward IV appointed Rivers Governor of the Prince of Wales' household, and Rivers went with the prince to Ludlow Castle. His duties included the administration of justice throughout the principality. When the king die
1 CONC d in 1483, he accompanied the Prince, now King Edward V, on the way back to London. However, they were waylaid by the Duke of Gloucester, who imprisoned the Earl and then had him beheaded at Pontefract Castle on June 25, 1483 as p
1 CONC art of his path toward becoming king (as Richard III).
1 CONT
1 CONT After his wife's death in 1473, Anthony was summoned to Parliament in her right as Baron Scales. On his death in 1483 the peerage fell into abeyance.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rivers was evidently quite learned, and no doubt had learned excellent French from his mother. He had met with the earliest English printer William Caxton when in exile in Bruges, and there in 1475-6 Caxton published Cordyale, or 
1 CONC Four last thinges, Rivers' English translation from the French of Jean Miélot of Les quattres choses derrenieres, itself a translation of the Cordiale quattuor novissimorum. After both of them had returned to England, one of the f
1 CONC irst, if not the first, books printed in England was Rivers' translation from French of the Sayings of the Philosophers, printed by Caxton at Westminster in 1477.[1]. Lambeth Palace Library has an manuscript illustration showing R
1 CONC ivers presenting a copy of this book to Edward IV.
1 CONT
1 CONT Anthony was succeeded by his brother, Richard, as Earl Rivers.
0 @N6000000002188376910@ NOTE Arthur III, Duke of Brittany
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Arthur III (in breton Arzhur III) (August 24, 1393 – December 26, 1458), known as the Justicier and as Arthur de Richemont, was Lord of Parthenay and titular Count (Earl) of Richmond in England and, for eleven months at the very e
1 CONC nd of his life, Duke of Brittany and Count of Montfort after inheriting those titles upon the death of his nephew.
1 CONT
1 CONT Belonging to the family of Montfort, he was a younger son of Duke John V and his third wife Joanna of Navarre. Just a year before his own death, Arthur succeeded his nephew Peter II as Duke. Arthur was also titular Earl of Richmon
1 CONC d; the earldom had often been granted to the Dukes of Brittany, but after the death of Arthur's father, the English refused to recognize his heirs as earls. Nevertheless, they continued to style themselves "Count of Richmond", whi
1 CONC le the English title was given to John, Duke of Bedford, Plantagenet (1389-1435) in 1414.
1 CONT
1 CONT Arthur was an important figure of the French court even before becoming duke of Brittany. He was one of the supporters of the charismatic Joan of Arc. Arthur was known for his tenacity and bad temper, characteristics that led to h
1 CONC is expulsion from the court in 1427. By 1435, however, he had regained influence, enabling him to orchestrate the Treaty of Arras between Charles VII of France and Philip III, duke of Burgundy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Arthur sided with the Armagnac faction against the Burgundians during the civil conflict in France between 1410 and 1414. He fought at the battle of Agincourt on October 2, 1914, where he was wounded and captured. He was released 
1 CONC by the English in 1420 and helped persuade his brother, Duke John, to sign the Treaty of Troyes. In 1422, the English created him Duke of Touraine. However, he subsequently returned to the allegiance of the Dauphin in 1424, was ma
1 CONC de Constable of France with support from Yolande of Aragon in 1425 and fought alongside Joan of Arc during her victory at the battle of Patay on June 18, 1429. He then helped arrange the Treaty of Arras (1435), which cemented the 
1 CONC peace between France and Burgundy leading to the eventual defeat of the English. He was commander of the French army at the Battle of Formigny on April 15, 1450, the next-to-the-last battle of the Hundred Years' War that sealed th
1 CONC e reconquest of Normandy.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriages
1 CONT
1 CONT Arthur was married three times but had no legitimate children although he had a natural daughter named Jacqueline who was legitimatized in 1443. He was succeeded as Duke of Brittany by his nephew Francis II, count of Étampes.
1 CONT
1 CONT His wives were as follows:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. married in Dijon on October 10, 1423 Marguerite of Burgundy (d. 1441), daughter of John the Fearless, Duke of Burgundy and widow of Dauphin Louis, Duke of Guyenne.
1 CONT    2. married in Nérac c. August 29, 1442 Jeanne d'Albret (d. 1444), daughter of Charles II, Count of Dreux
1 CONT    3. married on July 2, 1445 Catherine of Saint Pol (d. 1492), daughter of Peter I, comte de St-Pol
0 @N6000000002188026091@ NOTE Charles, Count of Maine
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles du Maine (Château de Montils-lez-Tours, 1414 – 1472, Neufvy-en-Touraine) was the third son of Louis II of Anjou and Yolande of Aragon.
1 CONT
1 CONT By his first marriage in 1434 to Cobella Ruffo (d. 1442), Countess of Montalto and Corigliano, he had one son:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Charles IV, Duke of Anjou (1436–1481)
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1437, he took up arms on behalf of Charles VII of France, participating in the capture of Montereau, and that of Pontoise in 1441. At this time, his brother René ceded to him the County of Maine. He continued to take part in Ch
1 CONC arles' campaigns, and was made Count of Guise (formerly a possession of his family) in 1444.
1 CONT
1 CONT By his second marriage to Isabelle of Saint-Pol (d. 1472), Countess of Guise, he had one daughter:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Louise (1445–1477, Carlat), married 1462 at Poitiers Jacques d'Armagnac, Duke of Nemours (d. 1477)
1 CONT
1 CONT He led the rearguard for King Louis XI at the Battle of Montlhéry.
1 CONT
1 CONT
