1 CONT Otto the Mild
0 @N6000000002188008201@ NOTE Albert II, Margrave of Meissen
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Albert II, the Degenerate (de: Albrecht II der Entartete) (b. 1240 – d. Erfurt, 20 November 1314), was a Margrave of Meissen, Landgrave of Thuringia and Count Palatine of Saxony. He was a member of the House of Wettin.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the eldest son of Henry the Illustrious, Margrave of Meissen by his first wife, Constantia of Austria.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life
1 CONT In 1265 the Margrave Henry III granted the Landgraviate of Thuringia and the Palatinate to Albrecht and the Margraviate of Landsberg in the Osterland to his younger brother Dietrich. Henry III kept from himself the Margraviates of
1 CONC  Meissen and Lausitz as a formal power over his sons.
1 CONT
1 CONT In June 1255 Albrecht married with Margaret of Sicily, the daughter of Emperor Frederick II. As a dowry the Pleissnerland was pledged to the house of Wettin. They had five children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Heinrich (b. 21 March 1256 - d. 25 January/23 July? 1282), inherited the Pleissnerland in 1274. 
1 CONT Frederick (b. 1257 - d. Wartburg, 16 November 1323), Margrave of Meisse
1 CONT Dietrich, called Dietzmann (b. 1260 - murdered Leipzig, 10 December 1307), Margrave of Lausitz. 
1 CONT Margaret (d. young, aft. 17 April 1273). 
1 CONT Agnes (b. 1264 - d. September 1332), married before 21 July 1282 to Heinrich I, Duke of Brünswick-Grubenhagen. 
1 CONT After what was at first a happy rule and marriage, Albrecht turned away from Margaret and began a passionate love affair with Kunigunde of Eisenberg. She bore him two children: a daughter, Elisabeth in 1264, and a son, Albrecht ("
1 CONC Apitz") in 1270.
1 CONT
1 CONT When she discovered the adultery and the illegitimate births, Margaret left Wartburg on 24 June 1270 and went to Frankfurt am Main where she died on 8 August of the same year. The two younger sons, Frederick and Dietzmann were loo
1 CONC ked after by their uncle, Dietrich, Margrave of Landsberg. Henry, the oldest, disappeared in Silesia in 1282.
1 CONT
1 CONT Albrecht married with Kunigunde in 1274 and legitimised their children. When Albrecht intended leave to Apitz the Landgraviate of Thuringia and compensating his sons from his first marriage only with the Osterland (who included th
1 CONC e inheritance of their mother) and the Palatinate County of Saxony, they began the war against their father. Frederick was captured by his father and was locked on Wartburg; however, he could escape one year later and continue the
1 CONC  war against his father together with Dietzmann. During this time, in 1284, their uncle Dietrich of Landsberg died, and four years later, in 1288, also died Heinrich the Illustrious, Albrecht's father. These deaths tightened the f
1 CONC amily disputes.
1 CONT
1 CONT By the death of his father Albrecht became a Margrave of Meissen, while his nephew Frederick Tuta -son of Dietrich of Landsberg- inherited the Margraviate of Lausitz, who was lost by Albrecht's son Dietzmann in that year. Shortly 
1 CONC after Frederick capture his father Albrecht in battle. By the Treaty of Rochlitz (1 January 1289) Albrecht obtain his freedom after the renunciation of big parts of his lands. He retain for himself Meissen, but he finally sold it 
1 CONC to Frederick Tuta. When after his death (1291) his cousins Frederick and Dietzmann arbitrarily took possession of his lands, Albrecht -with financial difficulties- was compeled to sell Thuringia in 1293 to the German King Adolf of
1 CONC  Nassau; in the contract, was estipulated the king could take posesion of the lands after Albrecht's death. In the sell, Albrecht included Meissen and Osterland as his fiefs, despite the fact they were in the hands of his sons. Th
1 CONC anks to this, Adolf's successor Albrecht I of Habsburg was able to take possession of these lands, claimed the contract of sell was legitimate and lawful.
1 CONT
1 CONT Kunigunde of Eisenberg died on 31 October 1286. Four years later, on 1 October 1290, Albrecht married thirdly with Elisabeth of Orlamünde, heiress of Nordhalben and widow of Hartmann XI of Lobdeburg-Arnshaugk. The same year, Apitz
1 CONC , Albrecht's son by Kunigunde, was formally legitimazed by the Emperor and created Herr of Tenneberg.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 11 April 1291 Apitz's younger full-sister, Elisabeth, married with Heinrich III of Frankenstein; the marriage was short-living and childless; Elisabeth died on 28 September 1293[1]. Three years later (9 October 1296), Apitz mar
1 CONC ried with another member of the Frankenstein family. These marriage, like his sister, was childless.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ten years later (24 August 1300) Albrecht's eldest surviving son, Frederick, married with Elisabeth of Lobdeburg-Arnshaugk, daughter of his step-mother; this make the final reconciliation between father and son. Five years later (
1 CONC 27 June 1305) Apitz of Tenneberg died, aged thirty-five. The death of his favorite son was a terrible blow to Albrecht. He was never recovered by the lost.
1 CONT
1 CONT Two years later, in 1307, Albrecht finally resigned the Landgraviate of Thuringia and the County Palatine of Saxony to his son Frederick in exchange for an annuity. He died seven years later in Erfurt, aged seventy-four.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT List of Margraves of Meißen 
1 CONT Wettin (dynasty) 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT ^ ThePeerage.com. In this site Elisabeth's death is cited after 23 April 1326 and her husband is named Heinrich II, not III. Also, the death of Elisabeth's brother Apitz is cited on 27 June 1301, four years before of the current d
1 CONC ate of the article. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Henry III Landgrave of Thuringia
1 CONT 1288–1292 Succeeded by
1 CONT Frederick I 
1 CONT Margrave of Meissen
1 CONT 1288–1292 
0 @N6000000002187834682@ NOTE Henry I of Bohemia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry VI of Carinthia (circa 1265 – 2 April 1335) was Count of Tyrol and Duke of Carinthia and Carniola from 1295 until 1335, titular King of Poland.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the son of Meinhard II of Gorizia-Tyrol and Elisabeth of Bavaria, a daughter of Duke Otto II. He originally ruled jointly with his brothers Otto and Louis, but outlived them.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was also the elected (1306) and actual King of Bohemia (1307-1310). After the murder of Wenceslaus III he was elected, as husband of Anna Premyslid, the daughter of Wenceslaus II. He was deposed in 1310 by John of Luxembourg.
1 CONT
1 CONT Since he was the last male heir of the Meinhardiner dynasty, he attempted to maintain their possessions, in which he ultimately failed. Even though he had supported Louis the Bavarian in his dispute about the imperial crown agains
1 CONC t the Habsburg Frederick the Handsome and Louis had assured him in 1330 that his daughters could succeed him, Louis reneged on his promise, and the Habsburg managed to take control over Carinthia after his death. His daughter Marg
1 CONC arete Maultasch succeeded him in Tyrol, but ultimately had to bequeath her land to the Habsburgs as well.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Meinhard II Duke of Carinthia
1 CONT Duke of Carniola
1 CONT 1295–1335
1 CONT jointly with Otto III and Louis Succeeded by
1 CONT Albert II and Otto IV
1 CONT Count of Tyrol
1 CONT 1295–1335
1 CONT jointly with Otto and Louis Succeeded by
1 CONT Margarete Maultasch
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Wenceslaus III King of Bohemia
1 CONT 1306 Succeeded by
1 CONT Rudolf I
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Rudolf I King of Bohemia
1 CONT 1307–1310 Succeeded by
1 CONT John
0 @N6000000006127682259@ NOTE Margarete von Tirol - Görz (Maultasch) (* 1318 in Südtirol; † 3. Oktober 1369 in Wien) war die Tochter von Herzog Heinrich von Kärnten und Tirol aus seiner Ehe mit Adelheid von Braunschweig und Gräfin von Tirol.Leben Margarete von
1 CONC  Tirol, genannt Margarete Maultasch1330 schloss Heinrich mit Kaiser Ludwig dem Bayern einen Vertrag, der ihm für seine Töchter die weibliche Erbfolge garantierte, wenn sie mit Genehmigung des Kaisers verheiratet würden. Die 1318ge
1 CONC borene Margarete wurde 1330 mit Johann Heinrich, dem Bruder des späteren Kaisers Karl IV. von Böhmen vermählt, der 1335 nach dem Tod Heinrichs die Regentschaft über Tirol übernahm, während Herzog Albrecht II. von Österreich das He
1 CONC rzogtum Kärntennach einer Übereinkunft mit Ludwig besetzte.Als Margarete im November 1341 ihren Gemahl Johann Heinrich aus Tirol vertrieb und am 10. Februar 1342 in Meran in Anwesenheit des Kaisers dessen Sohn Ludwig I. von Brande
1 CONC nburg heiratete, erregte dies in ganz Europa Aufsehen. Aus politischenGründen erkannte Papst Clemens VI. aber die Ungültigkeit der ersten Ehe nicht an. Darüber hinaus waren Margarete und Ludwig auch noch im dritten Grad verwandt. 
1 CONC Aus diesen Gründen wurden sie gebannt sowie ein Interdikt über das Land Tirol verhängt.Marsilius von Padua und William von Ockham verteidigten jedoch in Traktaten diese „Zivilehe“, die im Gegensatz zur ersten Ehe Margaretes mit Ki
1 CONC ndern gesegnet war. Ludwig der Brandenburger setzte sich als Regent in Tirol durch.Als der Luxemburger Karl (der spätere Kaiser Karl IV.) seine Ex-Schwägerin im März 1347 im Schloss Tirol belagerte, verteidigte Margarete dieses er
1 CONC folgreich.Erst 1359 wurde nach Vermittlung durch Albrecht II. der Bann gelöst. Nach dem Tod Ludwigs 1361 besuchte Margarete Karl IV. in Nürnberg; dabei bezeichnete der Kanzler Johann von Neumarkt die Herzogin als „Kriemhild“. Nach
1 CONC dem sowohl MargaretesGemahl als auch ihr Sohn Meinhard III. gestorben waren, überschrieb sie Tirol dem Habsburger Rudolf IV. und übergab diesem 1363 die Regierungsgewalt. Die Wittelsbacher fielen daraufhin in Tirol ein, auf das si
1 CONC e jedoch letztlich 1369 gegen einehohe finanzielle Entschädigung verzichteten.Ihre letzten Jahre verbrachte sie in Wien, wo ihr Leibgedingsitz einer ganzen Vorstadt den Namen „Margaretengrund“ gegeben haben soll (Bezirk Wien-Marga
1 CONC reten.)Der Beiname "Maultasch"Der Beiname „Maultasch“ wurde erstmals um 1366 in der dritten bayerischen Fortsetzung der „Sächsischen Weltchronik“ und 1393 in der „Österreichischen Chronik“ erwähnt. Er bedeutet soviel wie „Hure, li
1 CONC ederliches Weib“ und fand hauptsächlich in derpäpstlichen und böhmischen Propaganda Anwendung. Seit 1425 hat man dann den Beinamen wörtlich genommen und sich Margarete mit einem missgestalteten Mund vorgestellt. Die daraus folgend
1 CONC e Ansicht, Margarete sei allgemein von großer Hässlichkeitgewesen, ist aller Wahrscheinlichkeit nach falsch, denn Zeitzeugen haben Margarete als besonders schöne Frau beschrieben. Auch die Vorstellung, sie habe einen besonders ans
1 CONC tößigen Wortschatz besessen, ist historisch nicht haltbar.Eine dritte Erklärung leitet den Namen vom Lieblingsaufenthaltsort Margaretes, der im Volksmund „Schloss Maultasch“ genannten Burg Neuhaus in Terlan, ab. Demnach habe diese
1 CONC  Burg ihren volkstümlichen Namen von der unterhalb gelegenen "mala tasca"(Mausefalle) genannten Zollstation übernommen und sei in der Folge auf die Gräfin übergegangen.Der italienische Autor Filippo Villani bezeichnete sie um 1400
1 CONC  als „Medusa“. Die angebliche Belagerung der Burg Hochosterwitz geht auf die „Kärntner Chronik“ Jakob Unrests zurück; derartige Legenden wurden 1816 von Jakob Grimm in den „DeutschenSagen“ publiziert und durch Lion Feuchtwangers R
1 CONC oman (1923) neu popularisiert.
