1 CONT Principe di Benevento
0 @N6000000003949736631@ NOTE He was an important nobleman in the fight with the Byzantines and Moslems for control of the Mezzogiorno in the centuries after the collapse of Lombard and Carolingian authority in the peninsula. He established himself over almost
1 CONC  the whole of the southern half of Italia before his death in March 981.
1 CONT He co-reigned with his father from 943, when his grandfather Landulf I died, and with his brother Landulf III from 959. In 961, he and his brother became sole princes, though the elder Pandulf was by far the more domineering. The 
1 CONC Chronicum Salernitanum affirms the co-regency, however, and the principle of the indivisibility of the united Capua-Benevento as declared by Atenulf I in 900, when it says that is "the Beneventan principality was reigned in jointl
1 CONC y by Pandulf and Landulf under indivisible common jurisdiction." However, this system eventually collapsed and Pandulf ruled in Capua while Landulf ruled in Benevento.
1 CONT In Autumn 966, Pope John XIII led a Roman-Tuscan-Spoletan army against them, but Gisulf I of Salerno came to their rescue and no battle was given. The pope and Gisulf made a treaty at Terracina in 968. In that year, Pandulf's brot
1 CONC her John was made archbishop of Capua by John XIII. In 967, Emperor Otto I came down and gave him the duchy of Spoleto. Pandulf and Landulf took part in the campaign of 968, but Landulf retired in illness and died at Benevento lea
1 CONC ving two sons: Pandulf and Landulf di Sant'Agata, both later princes of Benevento. The Chronicum says that is, "he had the principality solely with his brother for eight years." Then he cheated his nephews out of their patrimony a
1 CONC nd made himself sole prince, associated with him his own son Landulf. He then rejoined the imperial campaign. In that year, Otto left the siege of Bari in the charge of Pandulf, but the allied duke was captured in the Battle of Bo
1 CONC vino (969) by the Byzantines. He was released later in the deal in which John Tzimisces, Byzantine emperor, gave Theophano in marriage to Otto's son Otto II. During his absence, the great principality had been administerd by the A
1 CONC rchbishop Landulf of Benevento and the young Landulf, with help from his mother Aloara.
1 CONT When Prince Gisulf of Salerno was deposed and removed from office by a religious insurrection led by his brother Landulf in 974, the Ironhead restored Gisulf as his vassal. Gisulf died heirless in 977 or 978 and Pandulf succeeded 
1 CONC in Salerno.
0 @N6000000003051248305@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Landulf II of Benevento", retrieved 13 Oct 07]
1 CONT Landulf II (died 961), called the Red, was the prince of Benevento and prince of Capua (as Landulf IV) from 939 or 940, when his father, Landulf I, first associated him with the government, his mother was Gemma, daughter of Athana
1 CONC sius of Naples. He may have been associated as early as 933, when his elder brother, Atenulf III, was made co-regent. His uncle Atenful II died in 940 and it is likely that Landulf served as a replacement.
1 CONT
1 CONT Whatever the case, when the elder Landulf died on 10 April 943, Landulf removed his elder brother Atenulf to Benevento and his uncle Atenulf's son Landulf to Capua. Fearing for their lives, the two fled to Guaimar II of Salerno an
1 CONC d Landulf the Red became sole prince. His first act was to continue the family policy of associated younger sons as co-princes in the government. He made his eldest son Pandulf co-prince. Other than that, Landulf made little attem
1 CONC pts to continue family policy of alliance with his fellow Lombards and détente with the Byzantines. He distanced himself from Constantinople, while trying not to enter into open warfare, and made several tries at reuniting the Lom
1 CONC bard principality of Salerno with the united Capua-Benevento. He abandoned imperial dating and dated from his own reign.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 946, he allied with John III of Naples to oust Gisulf I of Salerno, son of Guaimar. He was ambushed in a pass at La Cava by Mastalo I of Amalfi and the ousting failed. He soon broke his alliance with Naples and allied with Gisu
1 CONC lf to besieged Neapolitan Nola. In 950, he was called to the aid of Aligerno, abbot of Monte Cassino. His descendants would prove to be the worst persecutors of that great monastery. He followed up his Salernitan and Neapolitan fa
1 CONC ilures with successful campaigns against the gastald of Aquino, Atenulf Megalu, whom he exiled to the Docibilis II of Gaeta.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 955, Landulf made his biggest failure in supporting an Apulian revolt against Greek authority. He was forced to recognise Byzantine supremacy. The rest of his reign is less eventful, he failed in his two great ambitions: conque
1 CONC ring Salerno and opposing the Byzantines. He associated his second son, Landulf, with him in 959 and died in 961.
0 @N6000000000945074532@ NOTE Fyrste av Capua 961 - 981, 
1 CONT fyrste av Benevento 969 - 981 og
1 CONT fyrste av Salerno 978 - 981.
1 CONT Pandulf fikk ved sin fars død  i 961 fyrstedømmet Capua, men da hans bror, Landulf III,
1 CONT døde 968-69, fordrev han Landulfs arveberettigede sønn, Pandulf II, fra Benevento og regjerte
1 CONT deretter begge fyrstedømmene.
1 CONT Av Otto I fikk han markgrevskapet Spoleto og Camerino og knyttet i 978 til dette også
1 CONT fyrstedømmet Salerno. Han hadde dermed forenet de lombardiske fyrstedømmene.
1 CONT Han ødela imidlertid sitt verk ved før sin død å utpeke Capua og Benevento for sin
1 CONT eldste sønn Landulf IV som døde allerede i 982. Pandulf fikk Salerno, men ble senere
1 CONT fordrevet.
1 CONT Pandulf døde i mars 981.
0 @N6000000000945068728@ NOTE Fyrste av Benevento 961 -  969. 
1 CONT Landulf etterfulgte sin far 21.05.961 som fyrste av Benevento, mens broren
1 CONT Pandulf I hadde Capua.
0 @N6000000003051248315@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Landulf III of Benevento", retrieved 13 Oct 07]
1 CONT Landulf III (died December 968 or 969) was Prince of Capua (as Landulf V) and Benevento from 959 as co-prince with his father, Landulf II and brother Pandulf Ironhead, and from 961 only with his brother. His mother was Yvantia.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 961, Landulf II died and Landulf and his brother became sole princes, though the elder Pandulf was by far the more domineering. The Chronicum Salernitanum affirms the co-regency, however, and the principle of the indivisibility
1 CONC  of the united Capua-Benevento as declared by Atenulf I in 900, when it says Beneventanorum principatum eius filii Pandolfum et Landulfum bifarie regebant . . . communi indivisoque iure, that is "the Beneventan principality was re
1 CONC igned in jointly by Pandulf and Landulf under indivisible common jurisdiction." However, this system eventually collapsed and Pandulf ruled in Capua while Landulf ruled in Benevento.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 967, the Emperor Otto I came down to Rome and charged Pandulf with prosecuting a war against the Byzantine Empire in Apulia. Landulf took part in the subsequent campaign of 968, but he retired in illness and died at Benevento l
1 CONC eaving two sons: Pandulf and Landulf. The Chronicum says Landulf tenuit principatum una cum suo germanus annos octo, that is, "held the principality solely with his brother for eight years." Pandulf, however, cheated his nephews o
1 CONC ut of their patrimony, made himself sole prince. Both of Landulf's sons would later become princes of Benevento.
0 @N6000000000945074506@ NOTE Greve av Teano og fyrste av Capua fra 1022 til 1024. 
1 CONT Pandulf ble innsatt som fyrste av Capua i 1022 av keiser Henrik II. Samtidig ble den
1 CONT tidligere fyrsten, Pandulf IV, ført som fange til Tyskland. Da Konrad II løslot Pandulf IV i 1024,
1 CONT fordrev denne vår Pandulf kort tid senere.
0 @N6000000002322303971@ NOTE From 1496  would find Naples and Sicily ruled by viceroys for two centuries.
1 CONT The first of such was under control of Spain interested only in the use of
1 CONT the countries resources and gaining tax monies. In the first half of the 18th
1 CONT century , Naples and Sicily came under the influence of the Austrian
1 CONT Habsburgs as result of the War of the Spanish Succession. Then, Charles III
1 CONT of Spain  conquered Naples and Sicily which were then governed by the
1 CONT Spanish Bourbons as a separate kingdom. Then came Napoleon, see #1254.
