0 @N6000000002188342159@ NOTE Richard of Shrewsbury, 1st Duke of York
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT English Royalty
1 CONT House of York
1 CONT
1 CONT Armorial of Plantagenet
1 CONT Edward IV
1 CONT    Elizabeth of York
1 CONT    Edward V
1 CONT    Richard, Duke of York
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard of Shrewsbury, 1st Duke of York and 1st Duke of Norfolk (17 August 1473 – 1483?) was the sixth child and second son of King Edward IV of England and Elizabeth Woodville. He was born in Shrewsbury. He was a younger brother 
1 CONC of Elizabeth of York, Mary of York, Cecily of York, Edward V of England and Margaret Plantagenet (Princess of York). He was also an older brother of Anne of York, George Plantagenet, Duke of Bedford, Catherine of York and Bridget 
1 CONC of York.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was created Duke of York in 1474. From this time on, it became a tradition for the second son of the British sovereign to be Duke of York. On 15 January 1478, when he was about 4 years old, he married the 5-year-old Anne de Mow
1 CONC bray, 8th Countess of Norfolk, who had inherited the vast Mowbray estates in 1476. Because York's father-in-law's dukedom had become extinct when Anne could not inherit it, he was created Duke of Norfolk in 1481.
1 CONT The Two Princes Edward and Richard in the Tower, 1483 by Sir John Everett Millais, 1878, part of the Royal Holloway picture collection
1 CONT The Two Princes Edward and Richard in the Tower, 1483 by Sir John Everett Millais, 1878, part of the Royal Holloway picture collection
1 CONT King Edward V and the Duke of York in the Tower of London by Paul Delaroche
1 CONT King Edward V and the Duke of York in the Tower of London by Paul Delaroche
1 CONT
1 CONT His father died on 9 April 1483. Thus his brother Edward, Prince of Wales, became King of England and was acclaimed as such, and Richard his Heir Presumptive. This was not to last. Robert Stillington, the Bishop of Bath and Wells,
1 CONC  testified that Edward IV had agreed to marry Lady Eleanor Talbot in 1461. Lady Eleanor was still alive when Edward married Elizabeth Woodville in 1464. The Regency council under Richard Plantagenet, 1st Duke of Gloucester, conclu
1 CONC ded that this was a case of bigamy, invalidating the second marriage and the legitimacy of all children of Edward IV by this marriage. Under Richard's influence, both Edward and Richard were declared illegitimate and removed from 
1 CONC the line of succession on 25 June 1483. The Duke of Gloucester, as a surviving younger brother of Edward IV, became King Richard III.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Duke of York was sent to the Tower of London by King Richard in mid-1483. What happened to him and his brother—the Princes in the Tower—after that has been the subject of much speculation and debate. In the 1490s, Perkin Warbe
1 CONC ck claimed to be Richard, Duke of York, but he is generally considered to have been an imposter, and was labelled thus by the Tudor regime. Richard's might have been the smaller of two skeletons discovered in a chest in the Tower 
1 CONC in 1674, but there is as yet no conclusive (by modern investigative standards) evidence one way or the other.
1 CONT Contents
